questions.questionText:
Assume VHDL-2008 with ieee.std_logic_1164 and ieee.numeric_std. v is std_logic_vector(7 downto 0) := x"9C" and n is an integer:

n <= to_integer(signed(v(7 downto 4))) + to_integer(unsigned(v(3 downto 0)));

What value does n take?

Assume VHDL-2008 with ieee.std_logic_1164 and ieee.numeric_std. a = 200 and b = 90 are of type unsigned(7 downto 0), and t is unsigned(8 downto 0):

t    <= ('0' & a) + ('0' & b);
sum  <= t(7 downto 0);
cout <= t(8);

What are cout and to_integer(sum)?

Assume VHDL-2008 with ieee.std_logic_1164 and ieee.numeric_std. a = 3 and b = 5 are of type unsigned(3 downto 0), and d5 is unsigned(4 downto 0):

d5 <= ('0' & a) - ('0' & b);

What is d5, and what does d5(4) indicate?

Assume VHDL-2008 with ieee.std_logic_1164 and ieee.numeric_std. a, b and s are signed(7 downto 0), with a = −100 and b = −50:

s   <= a + b;
ovf <= (a(7) xnor b(7)) and (s(7) xor a(7));

What are to_integer(s) and ovf?

Assume VHDL-2008 with ieee.std_logic_1164 and ieee.numeric_std. a, b and y are signed(7 downto 0) and t is signed(8 downto 0):

t <= resize(a, 9) + resize(b, 9);
y <= "01111111" when t(8) = '0' and t(7) = '1' else
     "10000000" when t(8) = '1' and t(7) = '0' else
     t(7 downto 0);

What is to_integer(y) for (a, b) = (100, 50) and for (a, b) = (−100, −50)?

Assume VHDL-2008 with ieee.std_logic_1164 and ieee.numeric_std. acc is a signal of type signed(7 downto 0) initialised to 0, and ovf is a std_logic signal initialised to '0':

process (clk)
  variable s : signed(7 downto 0);
begin
  if rising_edge(clk) then
    s := acc + 60;
    if acc(7) = '0' and s(7) = '1' then
      ovf <= '1';
    end if;
    acc <= s;
  end if;
end process;

What are to_integer(acc) and ovf after three rising edges?

y is a std_logic signal driven by two tri-state assignments, with a = '0' and b = '1':

y <= a when en1 = '1' else 'Z';
y <= b when en2 = '1' else 'Z';

What is y for (en1, en2) = (0, 0), then (1, 0), then (1, 1)?
